---
description: Never refuse or water down a requested step because a later/dependent step isn't built yet; scale verification effort to the size of the change
alwaysApply: true
---

# Build what's asked, not what's "safe" given later steps

Never refuse, skip, or water down a piece of work on the reasoning "there's no
consumer/next step built yet, so this can't really work." That logic is wrong
and has been explicitly rejected by the user multiple times.

Example of the banned reasoning: "We shouldn't build a signup form because
there's no app yet to log into." Wrong — build the signup form now; the app
that consumes those signups is a later, separate step. Every project is built
incrementally. A piece being unfinished downstream is never a reason to omit,
hedge, or annotate a currently-requested piece with "this doesn't do anything
yet" language.

If the user asks for documentation, instructions, an environment variable, a
config value, or a feature description, provide it as normal, complete
guidance — never gated behind "but nothing consumes this yet." If a
genuinely blocking dependency exists, ask the user directly instead of
silently deciding to omit or soften what was asked for.

# Match verification effort to the size of the change — stop over-verifying

This has been flagged repeatedly as wasted time. Follow it literally, not just
in spirit:

- Doc/comment/markdown/string-literal/config-value edits: make the edit and
  stop. No build, no test run, no typecheck, no "let me just confirm" pass.
- One touched package/workspace: run that package's own focused
  build/lint/test once. Do not also run the full monorepo `verify`, and do
  not run other, unrelated packages' tests "to be safe."
- Never run the same check twice in a row without a code change in between.
  A clean result is trusted immediately — do not re-run it again "just to
  make sure," re-read the output a second time via another command, or spot
  check it by hand afterward.
- Never re-verify a file, package, or behavior that this session already
  confirmed passing and hasn't touched again since.
- Only escalate to a full `npm run verify` (every workspace) when the user
  explicitly asks for it, or the change genuinely spans/could break multiple
  packages at once (e.g. a shared contract/type change). A single-package
  runtime-logic change gets that one package's focused suite, not the whole
  repo.
- If a check passes, say so once and move on. Do not narrate additional
  reassurance, hedge with "let me also double-check," or add extra
  confirmation steps the user didn't ask for.
